WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.150
- This here is a test paper
from a fierce competition.

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.750
It is from the Harvard-MIT
Math Tournament.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.820
Now just known as HMMT,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.740
it is an annual competition
for high school students,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.720
with current Harvard and MIT students

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.290
writing the questions
and running the event.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.340
It is intended to encourage
an interest in math,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.470
but they don't go easy on anyone.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:25.890
It is very prestigious,

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.570
and attracts about 1,000
students from around the world.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.010
I tried some of the questions,

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.780
and let me just say they are very tough.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.260
It is an extension beyond
what is taught in schools.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.080
So sometimes to solve these,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.960
you need far-flung theorems,
tricks and techniques,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.560
many of which I've never
encountered before.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.840
And no, you don't get a calculator.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.040
Each year, there is a November
tournament held at Harvard,

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.900
and then a February or March
tournament held at MIT.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.830
The November one is supposed to be easier,

00:00:57.830 --> 00:00:59.460
but it's not easy at all.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.600
It's just less focused
on mathematical proofs

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.190
than the February one.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.820
Let me show you what some
of the questions look like.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.210
This is the November General Round paper,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.480
and there are 10 questions.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.470
It looks like they get
harder towards the end.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.180
Although the actual points
given for each question

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.970
is determined by a scoring algorithm,

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:21.530
which takes into account

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.260
how many people got that question correct.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.020
It starts off not so bad.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.110
The first question is about
four different-colored dominoes:

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:34.120
red-red, red-blue,
blue-red, and blue-blue.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.840
We're asked to find the number of ways

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.720
to arrange the dominoes
in a row, end to end,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.780
such that adjacent ends
have the same color.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.580
The answers provided on the website

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.920
say that the answer for this one is 4.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.690
From a little bit of trial and error,

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.600
and extrapolating all the possibilities.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.700
Question 2 is testing your ability

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.830
to manipulate an algebraic expression.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.760
The answers show that if you realize

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.960
it's useful to work
with the quotient rules,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.050
you can work out the
possibilities for the answer.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.060
But then, in my eyes, they
get a lot more difficult.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.810
Let's go down to number 7,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.190
and I'll read it out to you in full.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.040
We're told that there's this function,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.400
which is defined by this expression here

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.020
if x is not in the range between

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.900
minus cube root 2 and 0,

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.300
and 0 otherwise.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.600
So when x is in that range, it's 0.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.220
The sum of all real numbers x

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.490
for which f 10 of x is equal to 1

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.340
can be written like this:

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.130
a + b times root c over d,

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.460
where a, b, c, d are integers.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.750
d is positive, c is square-free.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.650
And the greatest common
denominator of a, b, and d is 1.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:52.650
Find 1000 a + 100 b + 10 c + d

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.700
In brackets, they say here that

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.700
f n of x is the function
f of x iterated n times.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:02.680
I couldn't keep up with their solution

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.050
that found this different
expression for f of x,

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.870
implying something else.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.160
And then they reverse-engineer
the quadratic formula.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.990
This is the rest of the solution here.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.990
And keep in mind, for this
paper of 10 questions,

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.570
you have 50 minutes.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.770
And the idea of working
through this in the time limit,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.680
I just thought,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.600
I just can't. I just can't.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.730
You can't what?

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.300
I can't. I can't even.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.810
You can't even what?

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.690
I just can't even.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:31.730
Moving on to question 8,

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.840
we have an interesting-sounding question

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.090
about an ant walking around on a circle.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:41.290
Then question 9, about a
cyclic convex pentagon,

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.830
and question 10, which is
suspiciously similar to number 7.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.980
It says that real numbers x, y, and z

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.910
satisfy these three expressions.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.480
The largest possible value of xyz

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.600
is a + b root c over d,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.160
where a, b, c, d are integers.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.380
Find 1000 a + 100 b + 10 c + d

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.230
That sounds very similar to the question

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.150
that we had in number 7,

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.770
except this time, instead
of being given a function,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.100
we have these three algebraic expressions.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.580
Now I was inspired to
take a look at this paper

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:20.200
by my friend PK, who runs
the channel "Dr. PK Math,"

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.200
and he's been solving some
of these problems lately.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.470
I spoke to him about
this exam in particular,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.750
and he's kindly uploaded
his fully-worked solution

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.130
of number 10.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.610
And after seeing how he did it,

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.840
I can actually understand it.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.560
PK has also uploaded his
solution for question number 7.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.900
So I'll link his videos
down in the description,

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.100
and you can check them out

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.150
if you wanna get a more complete view

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.140
of how the solutions are done.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:46.580
The first thing PK does

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.900
is to define a variable k

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.390
to be the largest possible value of xyz.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:54.790
He then takes a look at
this first expression,

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:56.780
and factors out the x.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:01.780
So you have x times 1
+ y + yz is equal to 1.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.500
Then take a look at the second expression.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.860
We could replace xyz with k,

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.880
and then take k over to that side.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.190
So rewriting this, we'd have y + yz

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.240
is equal to 2 minus k.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.370
This left-hand side is pretty similar

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.393
to what we have inside this bracket here,

00:05:21.393 --> 00:05:23.550
just with an additional 1.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:28.163
So if we wanted to know 1 + y + yz,

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.690
well, that would be equal
to 2 minus k, plus 1,

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.170
which would be 3 minus k.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.430
Then this would tell us that

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.780
x times 3 minus k is equal to 1.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:43.390
And we can can rearrange to get that

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:47.300
x is equal to 1 over 3 minus k.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.220
This is one of the three
important equations

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.120
that we'll need to solve the problem.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.360
We can do very similar
things to find y and z,

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:56.480
just by changing

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.350
which expression we
start that process with.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.183
Going through it,

00:06:00.183 --> 00:06:03.140
you'd get these as the
expressions for x, y, and z.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:04.860
Then if you multiplied them all together,

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:09.590
you'd get xyz, also known
as k, to be this expression.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:13.090
This can be multiplied out and rearranged

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.310
to give you a quartic for k.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:17.300
And if you managed to find the roots

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.250
of this quartic equation

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.710
through a combination of
guesswork, factorizing,

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.140
division, and the quadratic formula,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.990
you'd find that they are 1, 4,

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:32.320
and 5 plus or minus square root 17 over 2.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.110
This last root is in the form

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.090
that they told us about in the question.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:39.870
This a + b root c over d,

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:43.500
and k is the largest when we
take the positive of this.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.330
So we can find our a
to be 5, our b to be 1,

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.870
c, 17; and d is 2.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:52.770
Adding that all up in the way they want

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:54.410
would give you the answer,

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:58.000
which is written down here as 5,272.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.890
They do it a different
way in the solutions,

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.490
which doesn't involve solving a quartic,

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:03.400
but in my eyes,

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.240
their first step takes a much
greater leap of imagination

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:08.270
to think of.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.260
Anyway, this was just the General Round,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.500
and there are plenty more
rounds waiting for us.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.300
Here's the Theme Round.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.540
And this will be another
10 questions in 50 minutes.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.020
I'm not sure what the theme was here.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.550
Maybe it was to be as
mind-bending as possible,

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.917
because all the questions
start with this phrase:

00:07:24.917 --> 00:07:27.380
"Let n be the answer to this problem."

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:28.320
Good luck with that

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:31.470
if you can't find the
answers to the problems.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:35.050
Question 5 says, let n be
the answer to this problem.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:40.050
The polynomial x to the
n + a x squared + bx + c

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:45.530
has real coefficients
and exactly k real roots.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:49.120
Find the sum of the possible values of k.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.780
And then there's a Team Round.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.960
On here, we've got
questions such as number 5:

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.230
How many ways are there
to place 31 knights

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.980
in the cells of an 8 by 8 unit grid,

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:02.720
so that no two can attack one another.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.040
Note that a knight attacks another knight

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.530
if the distance between
the centers of their cells

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.410
is exactly root 5.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.480
Then there's something
called the Guts Round.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.460
And this seems probably
the most fun of the rounds.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:15.850
You're working in teams,

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:17.250
and it's one of those competitions

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.110
where the team sends a runner up

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.590
to go and get the questions.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:23.420
And they come in these groups of three,

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:25.230
and you don't get the
next set of questions

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:26.950
until you finish these ones.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:28.780
These are of varying difficulty,

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:30.740
and the first ones are pretty easy,

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:32.250
but I like number 3.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.860
It says, suppose all the
letters that spell "Harvard"

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.410
multiplied together,

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:40.840
is equal to the letters of
"MIT" multiplied together,

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:44.050
equal to h times m times m times t.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:46.700
And all of those are equal to 100.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.370
Then find the letters multiplied together

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:52.020
that make up "rad trivia."

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:53.580
It's a question of trying to make

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.180
some combination of these letters up here

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:58.440
to be left with these ones here.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.247
And the way it can be done is to do

00:09:00.247 --> 00:09:04.270
"Harvard" times "MIT" times "MIT"

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.880
divided by "HMMT."

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:11.880
That's 100, 100, 100 divided by 100.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.160
So it's 100 squared, which is 10,000.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.910
And if you cross out all the letters

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:19.760
that appear on the top and the bottom,

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.010
you get "rad trivia."

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:24.340
Now let's take a look at
the spring tournament.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.330
I believe it's usually held in February,

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.200
but last year was held in March, online.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:30.690
Instead of a General Round,

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:34.790
the individual questions here
are split into three topics.

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:37.240
We have have the Geometry Round,

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.260
with questions such as,

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:44.260
Two circles with radii 71 and
100 are externally tangent.

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:49.170
Compute the largest possible
area of a right triangle

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:53.830
whose vertices are each on
at least one of the circles.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:56.110
Next is the Combinatorics Round,

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:58.110
with questions such as:

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:02.020
A light pulse starts at a
corner of a reflective square.

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:04.400
It bounces around inside the square,

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.580
reflecting off the
square's perimeter n times

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.800
before ending in a different corner.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:12.510
The path of the light pulse, when traced,

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:17.510
divides the square into
exactly 2,021 regions.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.890
Compute the smallest possible value of n.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.020
And the last individual round for spring

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:25.670
is the Algebra and Number Theory Round,

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:26.780
where you'll find:

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.410
Compute the sum of all positive integers n

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:30.964
for which the expression

00:10:30.964 --> 00:10:34.530
n + 7 over the square root of n minus 1

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:35.390
is an integer.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:37.740
It starts off somewhat comprehensible,

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.210
but it devolves into very
complicated questions

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:41.350
that I'm not even confident

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.500
I'll be able to read out correctly.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.260
And then here's the Team Round
for the spring tournament.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.500
The big difference here
to the November tournament

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.710
is that these are proof type questions.

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:55.640
For example, show this or show that,

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:59.530
determine with proof the
value of this expression,

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:01.470
and to see what proof answers

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.570
are kind of expected to look like,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.350
let's take a look at the
solutions provided online.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:07.240
For each question,

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.580
there tends to be multiple
possible solutions.

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:11.910
I was starting to think that

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:14.690
whatever high school students
are able to ace these

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:16.630
must be extremely clever.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:20.300
Wikipedia has a list of
the winners from each year,

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:21.380
and taking a look at it,

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:23.650
I noticed that it wasn't uncommon

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:25.550
for the individual champion

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.460
to win multiple years in a row.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.680
For example, Luke Robitaille has won

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.150
in 2021, 2020 and 2018.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:34.020
So I looked him up,

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.760
and I found he actually
has a YouTube channel

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.470
where he solves math problems.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:39.090
Shall I say, much more advanced

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.110
than the ones I solve on my channel.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.140
I also looked up a lot of the other names,

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.300
and a big trend I saw

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.590
was that a lot of them have gone on to win

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:48.590
sometimes multiple gold medals

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:50.700
at the International Math Olympiad.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.430
So this competition attracts people

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:55.620
who are already performing
at the very top level.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.870
I found that most of the more recent names

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.810
were currently grad
students in mathematics,

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:04.640
either at MIT or Harvard
or other prestigious unis,

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:07.700
but then scrolling all the way
back to the first few years,

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.640
this guy Ricky won over 20 years ago.

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.620
So I wondered what he was up to now.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:14.500
And if this is the same person,

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.150
then he's currently an assistant professor

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:19.670
working in research mathematics.

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.180
And that wasn't uncommon either.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:24.540
A few names among the list
seem to be working in maths.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:25.630
And I guess after all,

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:27.090
the point of this competition

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.380
was to encourage interest in math

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.720
and to show what higher math looks like

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:33.490
to high school students.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:34.550
It doesn't surprise me

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.830
that the winners who had really
immersed themself in this

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.280
want to do more than just
be interested in math,

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:43.383
but really wanna turn it into
their life and their career.

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:45.830
Thanks for watching this video,

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:48.720
and I encourage you to go
and check out Dr. PK Math.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:51.610
He's got heaps of exam
type videos on his channel,

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:54.050
including the two solutions to questions

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.560
from this tournament.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.520
Thanks to my patrons for
making these videos possible,

00:12:58.520 --> 00:12:59.895
and a special shoutout

00:12:59.895 --> 00:13:02.520
to today's patron cat of the day, Tom.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.505
